Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:09:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 20/31] extensions: keep registered content scripts across a
 restart

chrome.scripting.registerContentScripts keeps a script across sessions
unless the extension says otherwise, and in QtWebEngine every one was
gone after a restart. ExtensionSystemQt::dynamic_user_scripts_store()
returned null, so the user-script manager had nowhere to write them.
An extension that registers its scripts once, on install or on a
setting change, lost them at the next start (villekivela/omaweb#686).

A regular profile now has the store Chrome has, in its storage path
under "Extension Scripts". It is made before the user-script manager,
which registers its key with the store when it is built. An
off-the-record profile has no store. Its loader refuses every extension
today, so nothing would reach one, but such a profile can still name a
storage path, and the store must not be opened there if that changes.

Chrome's store forgets an extension's scripts when the registry says it
was uninstalled, and Qt's uninstall only unloaded it, so a script came
back when the same extension was installed again. The extension manager
now tells the registry, and clears the URL patterns kept for the
scripts, which Chrome drops with the rest of the extension's prefs.
Every observer hears it, as in Chrome, so the service worker manager
also deletes the extension's worker registration. Unloading alone keeps
the scripts, as disabling does in Chrome.

An update that leaves persistAcrossSessions out keeps what the script
had, as the API documents. Chromium looked that up in the set it was
still filling, which is always empty, so such an update made the script
last only for the session. It now reads what persists from the loader
before the update. Chromium's main has the same code.

A new version of an extension starts with none of the old one's
scripts, as in Chrome, where the store is cleared when an update is
installed. Qt has no install step: an application replaces an unpacked
extension's files and loads the same path again. The extension system
now records the version whose scripts it keeps, and loading another
version forgets them and their URL patterns. Otherwise an extension
that registers the same ids at each start gets "Duplicate script ID"
and keeps running the old version's scripts.

A page an enabled extension's content scripts are for now waits until
the user-script manager has read them, as Chrome's UserScriptListener
holds it. That file is not in this tree, so UserScriptListenerQt is
Chrome's for one profile. Kept scripts are read from the store only
after the extension is enabled, and a page opened at once, such as a
restored tab, loaded without them. The listener waits for what the
manager reads: an extension's manifest scripts, and the URL patterns of
the scripts it kept if it still has the scripting or userScripts
permission, which is when the manager reads them. The manager signals
when every read is done, an unloaded extension included, so a page never
waits for a read that does not start.

The scripts themselves are in the store and their URL patterns in the
extension's prefs, which are written separately, so a crash between
the two writes loses them, as it does in Chrome.

The store is opened at the storage path the profile has when its
extension system is made. A path changed later is not followed, as
chrome.storage's is not.

A profile's preferences, the kept scripts' URL patterns among them,
are written to its storage path in the background, and a profile made
on that path reads them as it is made. One deleted and made again on
the same path, as the tests restart it, could read the file before the
last write of the one before it. It then started without the patterns,
so the user-script manager never read the scripts kept for them, and
its own next write dropped the patterns for good. PrefServiceAdapter
now waits for that write as it is destroyed, and before it makes a new
store for a profile set up again, which may come back to a path it used
before. The aarch64 build lost the race on every run, and x86_64 pinned
to one CPU in six runs of ten.

